          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Both patients in the opening vignette had haemoglobin 9.5 g/dL and MCV 70 fL wit</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Both patients in the opening vignette had haemoglobin 9.5 g/dL and MCV 70 fL with identical hypochromic microcytic smears. Which single laboratory finding most reliably distinguished iron deficiency anaemia from thalassaemia trait?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemoglobin concentration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemoglobin was identical at 9.5 g/dL in both patients and could not distinguish between the two diagnoses.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mean corpuscular volume</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MCV was 70 fL in both patients — identical — and therefore could not differentiate the two conditions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum ferritin</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Patient B's ferritin of 4 ng/mL confirmed depleted iron stores (IDA), while Patient A's ferritin of 68 ng/mL indicated intact stores, pointing to thalassaemia trait. Misdiagnosing Patient A led to harmful iron loading.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Peripheral smear morphology</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The peripheral smear appeared hypochromic and microcytic in both patients, making it unable to differentiate IDA from thalassaemia trait in this vignette.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Haemoglobin, MCV, and the smear were identical in both patients. Serum ferritin was the critical discriminator — 4 ng/mL in Patient B (IDA) versus 68 ng/mL in Patient A (thalassaemia trait)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The CBC and smear were identical — only serum ferritin revealed whether iron stores were depleted (IDA) or intact (thalassaemia trait)."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
