          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 20-year-old Sindhi woman has Hb 11.5 g/dL, MCV 64 fL, and a Mentzer Index of 1</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 20-year-old Sindhi woman has Hb 11.5 g/dL, MCV 64 fL, and a Mentzer Index of 11. Which investigation should be ordered next to confirm the suspected diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum ferritin and TIBC</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ferritin is typically normal in thalassaemia trait and would not confirm the diagnosis. The Mentzer Index below 13 already points away from iron deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A Mentzer Index below 13 favours thalassaemia trait. HPLC confirms beta-thalassaemia trait by demonstrating HbA2 above 3.5%. If HbA2 is normal with strong clinical suspicion, genetic testing for alpha-thalassaemia is indicated.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone marrow biopsy with Prussian blue stain</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bone marrow biopsy identifies ringed sideroblasts in sideroblastic anaemia. It is not required for screening thalassaemia trait.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Soluble transferrin receptor assay</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The sTfR assay helps distinguish iron deficiency from anaemia of chronic disease in inflamed patients. It is not the confirmatory test for thalassaemia trait.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A Mentzer Index below 13 (MCV ÷ RBC count) favours thalassaemia trait over IDA. The next step is HPLC, which confirms beta-thalassaemia trait when HbA2 exceeds 3.5%."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. HPLC is the standard confirmatory test — HbA2 above 3.5% confirms beta-thalassaemia trait."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
