          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What peripheral smear finding distinguishes sideroblastic anaemia from other cau</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What peripheral smear finding distinguishes sideroblastic anaemia from other causes of microcytic anaemia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>A dimorphic population of normal and microcytic, hypochromic cells</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Defective haem synthesis affects only a proportion of erythroblasts, producing two distinct red cell populations — normal cells alongside microcytic, hypochromic ones. This dimorphic pattern drives a characteristically high RDW.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Uniformly microcytic, hypochromic cells with a low RDW</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Uniform microcytosis with a low-normal RDW is characteristic of thalassaemia trait, where all red cells are equally small due to reduced globin chain synthesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Target cells with basophilic stippling</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Target cells are seen in thalassaemia and liver disease. Basophilic stippling suggests lead poisoning, not sideroblastic anaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pencil cells with marked anisopoikilocytosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pencil cells (elliptocytes) are characteristic of iron deficiency anaemia, not sideroblastic anaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Sideroblastic anaemia produces a dimorphic smear — normal red cells alongside microcytic, hypochromic cells — because haem synthesis is defective in only a proportion of erythroblasts. This drives a very high RDW, contrasting with the uniform microcytosis of thalassaemia trait."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The dimorphic smear with two distinct cell populations is a hallmark of sideroblastic anaemia, producing a very high RDW."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
