          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the stepwise diagnostic approach to microcytic anaemia, what should be assess</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the stepwise diagnostic approach to microcytic anaemia, what should be assessed after finding that the patient's ferritin is 45 ng/mL?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemoglobin electrophoresis by HPLC</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>HPLC is indicated when thalassaemia trait is suspected (disproportionately low MCV with high RBC count). The algorithm first evaluates for inflammation before considering thalassaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone marrow biopsy with Prussian blue stain</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bone marrow examination for ringed sideroblasts is reserved for when a dimorphic smear is identified. It is not the next step after finding a normal ferritin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Blood lead level</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A blood lead level is ordered when basophilic stippling and occupational exposure or paediatric pica are present, not as a routine next step after ferritin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inflammatory markers such as CRP and ESR</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. When ferritin is 12 ng/mL or above, iron deficiency is unlikely. The next step is to check for systemic inflammation (CRP, ESR) to evaluate for anaemia of chronic disease before considering thalassaemia trait or sideroblastic anaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The diagnostic algorithm begins with ferritin. When ferritin is not low (≥12 ng/mL), the next step is checking inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR) to assess for anaemia of chronic disease. HPLC and bone marrow come later in the sequence."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. A ferritin of 12 ng/mL or above rules out simple iron deficiency, making inflammatory markers the logical next step to evaluate for anaemia of chronic disease."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
